= 4
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Write the <complex potential of a two-dimensional potential flow> as $w=\phi+i\psi$, and the complex velocity as $q=w'=u-iv$. The lower solid wall has $\psi=0$. The upper solid wall and its continuing free streamline have constant $\psi$ equal to the far-field volume flux per unit depth, namely $a$. Consequently the potential domain is the infinite strip
$$
\boxed{0<\operatorname{Im}w<a.}
$$
The points $A,E$ correspond to $\phi\to-\infty$, $B,C$ to $\phi\to+\infty$, and $D$ is $w=ia$ by the chosen potential normalization.

In the <hodograph plane>, the horizontal wall has $q$ real positive, the vertical wall has $q$ imaginary positive because the physical velocity is downward, and the <fluid free boundary> has $|q|=1$. The upstream reservoir velocity tends to zero, the outgoing velocity is one, and the velocity at $D$ is $i$. Thus the hodograph is **the first-quadrant unit quarter disk**, with $A,E$ at $q=0$, $B,C$ at $q=1$, and $D$ at $q=i$.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For the potential strip, exponentiation followed by a <Möbius transformation> gives
$$
\zeta=-\frac1{1+e^{\pi w/a}}.
$$
Since $e^{\pi w/a}$ lies in the upper half-plane, so does $\zeta$. The map sends $A,E$ to $-1$, $D=ia$ to infinity, and $B,C$ to zero.

For the first-quadrant <hodograph plane>, successively square $q$, apply $(q^2-1)/(q^2+1)$, and then use a negative square:
$$
\zeta=-\left(\frac{q^2-1}{q^2+1}\right)^2.
$$
The first map takes the quarter disk into the upper half of the unit disk. The fractional transformation takes this to the second quadrant, whose negative square is the upper half-plane. The boundary points $0,i,1$ map to $-1,\infty,0$, as required. Uniqueness of the normalized <conformal map> therefore identifies the two expressions:
$$
\boxed{\frac1{e^{\pi w/a}+1}
=\left(\frac{(w')^2-1}{(w')^2+1}\right)^2.}
$$

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

On the free streamline choose $q=e^{-i\theta}$ with $-\pi/2\leq\theta<0$. The lower endpoint of this parameter interval is $D$, and $\theta\to0^-$ approaches $C$. The fractional expression in the map is
$$
\frac{e^{-2i\theta}-1}{e^{-2i\theta}+1}=-i\tan\theta.
$$
Hence $e^{\pi w/a}=-\csc^2\theta$, and the branch on the upper edge of the potential strip is
$$
w=\frac a\pi\log(\csc^2\theta)+ia.
$$
Differentiate to obtain $dw/d\theta=-2a\cot\theta/\pi$. Since $dw/dz=q$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{dz}{d\theta}=\frac1q\frac{dw}{d\theta}
=-\frac{2ae^{i\theta}\cot\theta}{\pi}.}
$$
The sign and interval are consistent with the free surface moving to the right and downward from $D$.

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

Separate the real and imaginary parts of the derivative and integrate from $D=(0,1)$:
$$
x(\theta)=-\frac{2a}{\pi}\int_{-\pi/2}^{\theta}\cos s\cot s\,ds,\qquad
y(\theta)=1-\frac{2a}{\pi}\int_{-\pi/2}^{\theta}\sin s\cot s\,ds.
$$
Because $\cos s\cot s=\csc s-\sin s$ and $\sin s\cot s=\cos s$, these integrals are
$$
x(\theta)=-\frac{2a}{\pi}\left[\log|\tan(\theta/2)|+\cos\theta\right],\qquad
y(\theta)=1-\frac{2a}{\pi}(1+\sin\theta).
$$
As $\theta\to0^-$, $x\to+\infty$ and $y\to1-2a/\pi$. This must equal the outgoing stream depth $a$, so
$$
\boxed{a=\frac{\pi}{\pi+2}.}
$$

\Image[2009-draining-flow.png]
{title=Draining free surface and its potential-strip and hodograph representations}

The solution has a vertical tangent at $D$ and approaches the horizontal line $y=a$ downstream, agreeing with both solid-wall and free-streamline conditions.

= 5
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Orient the contour so that the plus side is on the left; for a positively oriented simple closed contour this is its interior. If $\zeta(s)$ is a smooth arclength parametrization with $\zeta(0)=t$, define the <Cauchy principal value> by deleting a symmetric interval $|s|<\eta$ and taking $\eta\downarrow0$. This pairs the two singular sides at equal local arclength.

Near $t$, write $f(\zeta)=f(t)+[f(\zeta)-f(t)]$. The second term divided by $\zeta-t$ is regular because $f$ is analytic there. For the constant term, indent the contour on either side of $t$ by a small semicircle. Its limiting contribution to the Cauchy integral is $\pm f(t)/2$, while the remaining contour tends to its principal value. This proves the <Sokhotski–Plemelj theorem>:
$$
w_\pm(t)=\pm\frac12 f(t)+\frac1{2\pi i}
\operatorname{PV}\int_\Gamma\frac{f(\zeta)}{\zeta-t}\,d\zeta.
$$
In particular $w_+-w_-=f$ and $w_++w_-=g$ for the given integral equation.

Define $W=w$ inside and $W=-w$ outside. Its jump is $W_+-W_-=w_++w_-=g$, and it vanishes at infinity. Therefore
$$
W(z)=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_\Gamma\frac{g(t)}{t-z}\,dt.
$$
Indeed, the difference between the two sides has no jump, extends analytically across the smooth contour, and vanishes at infinity; <Liouville's theorem> makes it zero. Applying the <Sokhotski–Plemelj theorem> again,
$$
f=W_++W_-=\boxed{\frac1{\pi i}
\operatorname{PV}\int_\Gamma\frac{g(t)}{t-z}\,dt}.
$$
Thus the normalized closed-contour singular integral operator is its own inverse.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For an admissible <Laplace integral solution>, differentiation under the integral sign and <integration by parts> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
2w''+2zw'+w
&=\int_\Gamma(2\zeta^2+1)g\,e^{z\zeta}\,d\zeta
+2\int_\Gamma\zeta g\,\frac{d}{d\zeta}e^{z\zeta}\,d\zeta\\
&=[2\zeta g e^{z\zeta}]_{\partial\Gamma}
+\int_\Gamma\{(2\zeta^2-1)g-2\zeta g'\}e^{z\zeta}\,d\zeta.
\end{aligned}
$$
Choose endpoints for which the boundary term vanishes. Setting the density in braces to zero gives
$$
\frac{g'}g=\zeta-\frac1{2\zeta},\qquad
\boxed{g=A\zeta^{-1/2}e^{\zeta^2/2}.}
$$
Two suitable contours are the rays $\Gamma_+:0\to+i\infty$ and $\Gamma_-:0\to-i\infty$, with a continuous square-root branch on each ray. The Gaussian decays at infinity, and $\zeta g=O(\zeta^{1/2})$ tends to zero at the finite endpoint. The integrals converge for every finite $z$.

To check independence rather than merely exhibit different paths, define
$$
J_0=\int_0^\infty s^{-1/2}e^{-s^2/2}\,ds>0,\qquad
J_1=\int_0^\infty s^{1/2}e^{-s^2/2}\,ds>0.
$$
With unit normalization, the two solutions satisfy $w_\pm(0)=e^{\pm i\pi/4}J_0$ and $w_\pm'(0)=\pm i e^{\pm i\pi/4}J_1$. Their <Wronskian> at zero is $-2iJ_0J_1\ne0$, so they form an independent pair.

Strictly, the printed necessity claim requires an injective contour-transform class as well as vanishing endpoint terms. An arbitrary contour representation may contain a <null weight in a contour Laplace representation>, so a nonzero solution alone does not force every representing $g$ to have the displayed form. The calculation constructs the canonical density and two valid independent solutions; it does not assume uniqueness for unrestricted contour densities.

= 6
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}
{c}

Extend $f$ to be zero for $x<0$. Define $h(x)=0$ for $x>0$ and
$$
h(x)=\int_0^\infty K(x-t)f(t)\,dt\quad(x<0).
$$
Then the full-line equation is $K*f=f+h$. Use the <Fourier transform> convention
$$
\bar f(k)=\int_0^\infty e^{ikx}f(x)\,dx,\qquad
\bar h(k)=\int_{-\infty}^0e^{ikx}h(x)\,dx.
$$
Under the corresponding growth bounds, the positive-support transform is analytic above a horizontal line and the negative-support transform below a horizontal line. If both functions have suitable subexponential growth, the basic domains are respectively the upper and lower half-planes; exponential decay can enlarge these to overlapping strips. The <convolution theorem> gives $(\bar K-1)\bar f=\bar h$.

The <Wiener-Hopf method> factors $\bar K-1$ into functions analytic and nonzero in the respective domains. After multiplicative or additive separation, the two sides continue to one entire function, whose growth determines a polynomial by <Liouville's theorem>. Real-axis zeros must be assigned consistently with the inversion contour, rather than divided out without specifying a side.

For $K=e^{-|x|}$, direct integration gives $\bar K=2/(1+k^2)$, so
$$
\frac{1-k^2}{1+k^2}\bar f=\bar h.
$$
Rearrange the <Wiener-Hopf factorization> as
$$
\frac{k^2-1}{k+i}\bar f=-(k-i)\bar h.
$$
The left side has the positive-support analytic continuation and the right side the negative-support one. Their $O(1)$ growth, from the supplied $O(k^{-1})$ bounds, makes the common continued function a constant $C$. Consequently
$$
\bar f=C\frac{k+i}{k^2-1},\qquad \bar h=-\frac C{k-i}.
$$
For this kernel, $h$ on the negative axis is a multiple of $e^x$, so the latter transform is analytic for $\operatorname{Im}k<1$. The common strip can therefore be chosen as $0<\operatorname{Im}k<1$. In particular **the inversion line must pass above both real poles $k=\pm1$** of $\bar f$.

Set $C=iA$. Then
$$
\bar f=A\frac{ik-1}{k^2-1}.
$$
This is the transform, on $\operatorname{Im}k>0$, of
$$
\boxed{f(x)=A(\cos x+\sin x),\qquad x>0.}
$$
Equivalently, <Fourier inversion> closed below the inversion line for $x>0$ picks up the two real poles with clockwise orientation.

As a direct check, let $G(x)=\int_0^\infty e^{-|x-t|}f(t)\,dt$. Since $(d^2/dx^2)e^{-|x|}=e^{-|x|}-2\delta_0$, the equation $G=f$ gives $f''=-f$. At zero, differentiation of the integral gives $G'(0)=G(0)$, so $f'(0)=f(0)$; this sets the sine and cosine coefficients equal. Direct substitution, or the transforms above, then verifies the full integral equation, not merely its differentiated form.
